russian forces are stepping up their  offensive to take territory in the  eastern region of donbass kiev says the  main city still under ukrainian control  severo donetsk is coming under heavy  bombardment a local governor accused  moscow of engaging in a scorched earth  approach and intentionally trying to  destroy the city  in kiev the polish president has become  the first foreign leader to address the  country's parliament in person since the  war  began show of wartime solidarity between  neighbours  polish president anja duda became the  first foreign leader to speak to the  ukrainian parliament in person since the  war began  duda stressed his support for ukraine's  bids to join the eu and said that any  territorial concessions to russia would  be a blow for the entire western world  i want to say clearly  only ukraine has the right to decide its  future  there can be no negotiations and  decision-making behind ukraine's back  nothing can be decided about ukraine  without ukraine absolutely not this is  an iron principle  [Applause]  ukrainian president vladimir zelinsky  has said that only a diplomatic solution  will bring a definitive end to the  conflict but the government in kiev has  repeated it will not accept any  ceasefire that would require giving up  territory  on the battlefield russia's offensive to  capture the donbass grinds on with  shelling continuing along the front line  russian forces have intensified their  efforts to surround severo donetsk the  main city still held by ukraine in the  luhansk province  with fierce fighting raging across the  donbas neither side looks ready to  accept a compromise just yet  ndw's max sander joins me now from kiev  max the fighting in donbass has shifted  to the city of savara donetsk in recent  days ukraine is struggling to hold on to  it could that city become another  mariupol  right terry so analysts and military  experts have been voicing concerns over  the last couple of days that we would  see some more intense fighting along the  front lines in the donbass region the uh  industrial region of the southeast of  ukraine and this appears to have been  particularly true in and around the city  of seville dogs  where we've seen some intensive shelling  some air strikes and according to the  ukrainian military attempt by the  russian forces to take neighboring towns  in order to continue their assault on  the city which according to the  ukrainian military has not succeeded so  at the moment it doesn't seem that any  side is making any particular land gains  the russians are meeting fierce  resistance by the ukrainian side but the  situation in the town or in the city  itself is is dire as we can imagine  seville donetsk had about a hundred  thousand inhabitants prior to the war  and from what we're hearing there's at  the moment just one hospital in  operation with three doctors taking care  of the population of the entire city so  we can imagine the situation there is  deteriorating as we speak  tell us max why is siberia donette so  important for the russians  well the city  is important um  one of the reasons is that  in the first stages of the invasion um  russia tried to take to take kiev to  take the entire country  that was out of reach of moscow there  was a this was a military  not a success so they tried to um chip  away at other parts of the country and  the turn shift their focus south east to  the the regions held by russian  state-backed rebels and in particular in  the regions done uh in donetsk  province's donesk and luhansk and uh  seville donetsk is one of the last  remaining cities  in  the luhansk province that is in control  by the ukrainians so uh taking this town  for the russians would mean that they  would take uh the entire almost the  entire province and that they would have  control over that area and that would  bring them closer to their acclaimed  goals that they uh announced at this uh  the second stage in in later stages of  the invasion  what about the local residents there in  spirit donetsk  you mentioned that before the war there  were around a hundred thousand people  there  are many of them still left there  that is very hard to tell  what exactly is happening there at the  moment how many people have stayed but  this morning a police report was issued  that there had been a shelling of  residential buildings and an elderly man  was hurt and had to be brought to the  hospital so there are people still  living there  the luhan's governor sergey heidi said  that  russia was intentionally trying to  destroy the city and was applying a  tactic of scorched earth and that brings  us back to this mario pole comparison  this is something that we've seen  by russian forces done in chechnya in  syria and yeah we can imagine that the  people  and in mario paul  and we can imagine the people left in  the city  for them the situation is  unbearable  max thank you very much our  correspondent max sander there in kiev  frank ludwig is our regular military  analyst and joins me now from oxford in  the uk frank russia says it now controls  95  of luhansk province in ukraine is that  the case and if so how significant is  this development  well good morning terry i suppose it  depends on what one means by control  since there is  a intense battle going on as your  reporter said earlier around that city  of severo donetsk and its twin city  leschansk which is just just alongside  it  uh control would be the wrong word so  the russians are moving to surround that  city we will see over the next weeks  something like what happened in  mariappol  the difference is that  sivera donetsk is prepared for this as  mario paul wasn't  ukrainian ukrainian's been fortifying it  now for about seven years they knew  exactly what was going to happen  it's very reasonable to assume it was  going to happen and it has  and this will be a very very tough fight  however that said they are moving to  surround it they are making incremental  gains the russians and uh this battle  will go on for some time  okay so there's that battle that's uh  shaping up to be quite significant uh  what beyond that do you think is the  next step that the russian military is  likely to take in ukraine  well terry it's interesting that you  mentioned earlier that the russians have  claimed to control 95 percent of of uh  luhans province  one wonders whether that is now the  limit of their operational objective  it looks like it at the moment in  addition to what they've already gained  in the west around herself and my apple  of course and what of course they lost  around kharkiv  so what we can expect is  what's happened we got a little bit of  this from putin's speech a couple of  weeks ago it's very much a narrowing of  those objectives  uh  and focusing now on on that area with an  artillery  artillery-centred battle that's where  we're going now  so do you see an end game shaping up uh  in any of this frank  it was interesting as well wasn't it  president zielenski talked about or has  talked about the last few days is this  being the final stage of the war of  course it isn't  and what we could say i suppose it's the  end of the beginning and we're seeing  the rest of the war which could go on  for months or years  taking shape with the decisive battles  or the or the battles in any way  predominantly taking place down in this  southeast quadrant of the country which  we've known for some time  and unless negotiations are going to  intrude and there's no evidence that  they are ukraine has already said that  it will not accept anything short of the  recovery of its territory this war of  course is going to to grind on  in the short to medium term we're going  to see a big battle around siberia  donetsk the russians cannot sustain this  limited very limited momentum they are  achieving  and we will see things start to settle  down into something that looks like  status within the next few weeks  certainly i think before  the end of july middle of july  frank thank you very much for your  assessment that was frank ludwig  military analyst talking to us from  oxford  thank you terry  well russia has been accused of  committing war crimes during its  invasion of ukraine since the war began  its forces have targeted homes schools  and hospitals the scale of the  atrocities only becomes clear in areas  liberated by ukrainian forces dw's  matias berlinga went to one village in  the east where russian soldiers have  recently been forced out  a life in ruins switlana axionava has  just returned to what was her home  i can't say anything  a shell hit her house two days ago  this is what is left now we have nowhere  to come back to  people living here a message scribbled  on the door when the russian troops  arrived this village was occupied in the  very beginning of the war the russians  were here for two months and just three  weeks ago the ukrainians took it back  silana says that during those two months  she didn't even see the russian soldiers  that's because the fighting was so heavy  she never left her basement  the last night was the worst we thought  this is it  the house will collapse above us and  bury us in the basement our pets were  with us a dog and a cat  i came here to get the cat but i can't  find it  after the ukrainian army reclaimed the  village almost all inhabitants left for  the city of kharkiv as the fighting  continues  the russian positions are just beyond  the next village five kilometers away  most likely the shell that hit her house  came from there  if it doesn't collapse and isn't hit  again maybe we can come back and rebuild  it but i told my husband i would even  live in the basement as long as it is  home  for now she will go back to the city and  wait for the fighting in the area to end  the war in ukraine is also likely to  dominate the agenda of the world  economic forum getting underway in davos  switzerland today ukrainian president  